						<div id="siteSub" class="noprint">From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia</div>
					</div>
					<div id="contentSub"><div id="mw-content-subtitle"></div></div>


					<div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang="en" dir="ltr"><div class="shortdescription nomobile noexcerpt noprint searchaux" style="display:none">Ring worn by members of the United States Order of the Engineer</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><a href="/wiki/File:Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_(Macro).jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_%28Macro%29.jpg/220px-Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_%28Macro%29.jpg" decoding="async" width="220" height="161" class="mw-file-element" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_%28Macro%29.jpg/330px-Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_%28Macro%29.jpg 1.5x, //upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_%28Macro%29.jpg/440px-Order_of_the_Engineer_Ring_%28Macro%29.jpg 2x" data-file-width="2000" data-file-height="1468" /></a><figcaption>An example of the stainless steel Engineer's Ring issued by the <a href="/wiki/Order_of_the_Engineer" title="Order of the Engineer">Order of the Engineer</a></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><a href="/wiki/File:Order_of_the_Engineer_ring.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Order_of_the_Engineer_ring.jpg/220px-Order_of_the_Engineer_ring.jpg" decoding="async" width="220" height="195" class="mw-file-element" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Order_of_the_Engineer_ring.jpg 1.5x" data-file-width="234" data-file-height="207" /></a><figcaption>The ring is worn on the little finger of the dominant hand.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The <b>Engineer's Ring</b> is a ring worn by members of the <a href="/wiki/Order_of_the_Engineer" title="Order of the Engineer">Order of the Engineer</a>, a <a href="/wiki/Fellow#Learned_or_professional_societies" title="Fellow">fellowship</a> of <a href="/wiki/Engineer" title="Engineer">engineers</a> who must be a certified <a href="/wiki/Professional_Engineer" class="mw-redirect" title="Professional Engineer">Professional Engineer</a> or graduated from an accredited engineering program (or be within one academic year of graduation to participate).<sup id="cite_ref-faq_1-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-faq-1"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>1<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> The ring is usually a <a href="/wiki/Stainless_steel" title="Stainless steel">stainless steel</a> band worn on the <a href="/wiki/Pinky_ring" title="Pinky ring">little finger</a> of the dominant hand.<sup id="cite_ref-order_pdf_2-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-order_pdf-2"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>2<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> This is so that it makes contact with all work done by the engineer. Rings used to be cast in iron in the most unattractive and simple form to show the nature of work. The ring symbolizes the oath taken by the wearer, and symbolizes the unity of the profession in its goal of benefitting mankind. The stainless steel from which the ring is made depicts the profession's strength.<sup id="cite_ref-order_pdf_2-1" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-order_pdf-2"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>2<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Starting in 1970,<sup id="cite_ref-faq_1-1" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-faq-1"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>1<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> it was inspired by the <a href="/wiki/Iron_Ring" title="Iron Ring">Iron Ring</a>, which is part of the original Canadian <a href="/wiki/Ritual_of_the_Calling_of_an_Engineer" title="Ritual of the Calling of an Engineer">Ritual of the Calling of an Engineer</a> ceremony first attended a century ago in 1922. Canadian engineers about to graduate are invited to attend the ring ceremony by the <a href="/wiki/Ritual_of_the_Calling_of_an_Engineer#Management" title="Ritual of the Calling of an Engineer">Corporation of the Seven Wardens</a> in order to take the oath known as the Obligation of the Engineer.<sup id="cite_ref-3" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-3"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>3<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> Only those who have met the standards of <a href="/wiki/Professional_engineering" class="mw-redirect" title="Professional engineering">professional engineering</a> training or experience can accept the Obligation, which is voluntarily received for life.
</p>
<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" />
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="The_Obligation_of_the_Engineer">The Obligation of the Engineer</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Engineer%27s_Ring&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: The Obligation of the Engineer"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The required oath, taken immediately before accepting the Engineer's Ring, is known as "The Obligation of the Engineer" and is as follows:
</p>
<style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1211633275">.mw-parser-output .templatequote{overflow:hidden;margin:1em 0;padding:0 32px}.mw-parser-output .templatequote .templatequotecite{line-height:1.5em;text-align:left;padding-left:1.6em;margin-top:0}</style><blockquote class="templatequote"><p>I am an Engineer.
</p><p>In my profession I take deep pride.
To it I owe solemn obligations.
</p><p>As an engineer, I, (full name), pledge to practice Integrity and Fair Dealing, Tolerance, and Respect,
and to uphold devotion to the standards and dignity of my profession,
conscious always that my skill carries with it the obligation to serve humanity
by making best use of the Earth's precious wealth.
</p><p>As an engineer, I shall participate in none but honest enterprises.
When needed, my skill and knowledge shall be given without reservation for the public good.
</p><p>
In the performance of duty, and in fidelity to my profession, I shall give the utmost.</p></blockquote>
<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Engineer%27s_Ring&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: See also"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
